    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve for \\( \\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) \\) given that \\( \\det (\\mathbf{M}) = -2 \\), we can use the property of determinants that states the determinant of a matrix raised to a power is equal to the determinant of the original matrix raised to that power. In mathematical terms, this property is expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^n) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^n\n\\]\n\nHere, \\( n = 4 \\). Therefore, we substitute the given value \\( \\det \\mathbf{M} = -2 \\) into the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (-2)^4\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\( (-2)^4 \\):\n\n\\[\n(-2)^4 = (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate \\( (-2) \\times (-2) \\):\n\n\\[\n(-2) \\times (-2) = 4\n\\]\n\nNext, we take the result and multiply it by \\( -2 \\):\n\n\\[\n4 \\times (-2) = -8\n\\]\n\nFinally, we take the result and multiply it by \\( -2 \\) again:\n\n\\[\n(-8) \\times (-2) = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( \\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
